WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:14.900 --> 00:00:19.532
What I'd like to discuss is the birth of gene targeting,

00:00:19.533 --> 00:00:22.666
particularly our contribution to this field.

00:00:22.667 --> 00:00:29.032
First of all, what is gene targeting? It's a method, essentially, of being able to

00:00:29.033 --> 00:00:33.932
change any gene in any conceivable manner in an organism.

00:00:33.933 --> 00:00:36.499
And our particular organism is the mouse.

00:00:36.500 --> 00:00:41.499
And so what we want to do, the mouse has many genes, 30,000 genes.

00:00:41.500 --> 00:00:45.232
And this allows one to selectively inactivate a particular gene

00:00:45.233 --> 00:00:49.199
and for example, if a little finger disappears

00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:51.432
then we know when the program for making a little finger is.

00:00:51.433 --> 00:00:57.032
And then that way, be able to deduce, essentially, what each gene

00:00:57.033 --> 00:01:03.866
is doing by what outcome to the mouse is...when we modify a particular gene.

00:01:03.867 --> 00:01:05.966
So how is this done?

00:01:05.967 --> 00:01:12.099
The experiments actually started back in the 1970's.

00:01:12.100 --> 00:01:19.999
Richard Axel and Wigler had shown, essentially, if you make a

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:25.266
precipitate of DNA and put them on top of cells, the cells eat the DNA

00:01:25.267 --> 00:01:28.832
and then a certain amount of it would then go

00:01:28.833 --> 00:01:30.532
into the genome and be functional.

00:01:30.533 --> 00:01:36.766
For example, if a cell is thymidine kinase minus, this is an enzyme that's required for

00:01:36.767 --> 00:01:44.299
thymidine uptake. So if that gene isn't there, they can supply it exogenously.

00:01:44.300 --> 00:01:47.966
And they add...make a precipitate, give it to the cells, and about

00:01:47.967 --> 00:01:52.899
1 in a million cells actually, then, acquires this gene in functional form

00:01:52.900 --> 00:01:56.732
and becomes thymidine kinase positive.

00:01:56.733 --> 00:02:01.866
So, first of all, we thought, well, perhaps, if we actually made needles

00:02:01.867 --> 00:02:05.332
very small needles and they were like microinjection needles

00:02:05.333 --> 00:02:11.166
like a hypodermic, we could actually direct the hypodermic right into the nucleus of the cell

00:02:11.167 --> 00:02:17.399
and thereby plant the DNA into the nucleus and maybe that would work much more efficiently.

00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:22.932
And that turned out to work...instead of the efficiency being 1 in a million

00:02:22.933 --> 00:02:28.232
it is now 1 in 3.  So 1 in 3 cells acquire the cell in functional form.

00:02:28.233 --> 00:02:31.966
But the DNA went randomly into the genome

00:02:31.967 --> 00:02:34.566
it didn't go into a very specific place.

00:02:34.567 --> 00:02:39.599
So we repeated those experiments and what we noted is if

00:02:39.600 --> 00:02:44.366
we put in multiple copies of the same DNA,

00:02:44.367 --> 00:02:49.566
what we found is that, again, that...all of that DNA went randomly into the genome

00:02:49.567 --> 00:02:54.399
but something very unexpected was seen.  DNA has a direction; you read

00:02:54.400 --> 00:03:00.732
it from say left to right. And so, what we found is that all the DNA molecules

00:03:00.733 --> 00:03:03.932
were lined up next to each other in what we call a concatemer,

00:03:03.933 --> 00:03:07.666
a head to tail concatemer, they're all in the same direction.

00:03:07.667 --> 00:03:12.666
Now, randomly, that's impossible, because we would put in a thousand copies

00:03:12.667 --> 00:03:16.666
and a thousand copies would all be head to tail, head to tail, head to tail.

00:03:16.667 --> 00:03:22.032
So there were only two possibilities for how this could happen.

00:03:22.033 --> 00:03:28.332
One is that that the...one would act like a template and then like a sausage machine

00:03:28.333 --> 00:03:33.299
and then turn out more and more copies and it would all come out as one

00:03:33.300 --> 00:03:35.999
large concatemer, again head to tail.

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:40.266
The other is by a process called homologous recombination.

00:03:40.267 --> 00:03:46.932
Where in essence, two molecules which have the same sequence can be split

00:03:46.933 --> 00:03:50.966
and be put together again and then again would have a head to tail concatemer

00:03:50.967 --> 00:03:53.299
by homologous recombination.

00:03:53.300 --> 00:03:57.732
And we were able to prove that, indeed, it was homologous recombination.

00:03:57.733 --> 00:04:02.032
First of all, that told us that the cells had homologous recombination.

00:04:02.033 --> 00:04:05.166
Second, it told us it was actually fairly efficient.

00:04:05.167 --> 00:04:07.932
You add a thousand molecules that are all stitched together by

00:04:07.933 --> 00:04:11.832
homologous recombination. So that was quite remarkable.

00:04:11.833 --> 00:04:15.832
The other thing that was remarkable is that we were using fibroblasts.

00:04:15.833 --> 00:04:19.399
These are cells, for example, that are present in our skin.

00:04:19.400 --> 00:04:24.832
And that was unusual because, people previously knew about

00:04:24.833 --> 00:04:28.866
homologous recombination, but they thought it had to do with sex.

00:04:28.867 --> 00:04:34.066
It had to do with parents, you know you always get a chromosome

00:04:34.067 --> 00:04:39.432
from your mother and a chromosome from your father and then instead of getting

00:04:39.433 --> 00:04:42.932
a whole chromosome yourself from one from your father and mother

00:04:42.933 --> 00:04:47.766
then they're split into many, many pieces and stitched together again by

00:04:47.767 --> 00:04:52.366
homologous recombination so that you get a much more variation essentially.

00:04:52.367 --> 00:04:53.466
Instead of getting a whole chromosome,

00:04:53.467 --> 00:04:55.699
you get a chromosome that's made up of parts

00:04:55.700 --> 00:04:58.266
from both your father and your mother.

00:04:58.267 --> 00:05:03.399
And then that way the variation of gene copies of gene variation that you get from

00:05:03.400 --> 00:05:07.099
your two parents is much greater than if you got a whole block of chromosome,

00:05:07.100 --> 00:05:09.032
one chromosome from your father and so on.

00:05:09.033 --> 00:05:13.699
So it mixes up and so that makes every sibling are different from another

00:05:13.700 --> 00:05:17.599
and simply the combination of genes  that you're acquiring from your father and your mother.

00:05:17.600 --> 00:05:20.866
But we were seeing it in skin cells.

00:05:20.867 --> 00:05:24.999
Fibroblasts which were derived from, for example, skin.

00:05:25.000 --> 00:05:27.399
And so that wasn't expected.

00:05:27.400 --> 00:05:33.432
So what that told us is that the machinery is there to do homologous recombination

00:05:33.433 --> 00:05:35.799
in any cell of the body.

00:05:35.800 --> 00:05:39.832
So that was the beginning of gene targeting.

00:05:39.833 --> 00:05:44.799
Now, we wanted to go to the next step.  We not only wanted simply to

00:05:44.800 --> 00:05:49.099
have homologous recombination between exogenous DNA molecules

00:05:49.100 --> 00:05:53.866
but we wanted to be able to have homologous recombination between a chosen gene

00:05:53.867 --> 00:05:57.932
that we're introducing from the outside that we've modified some way

00:05:57.933 --> 00:06:01.599
and then put it in the cell, it would find, essentially,

00:06:01.600 --> 00:06:05.066
its cognates of the same sequence in the genome

00:06:05.067 --> 00:06:08.966
exchange information with it, and then any modification that you could

00:06:08.967 --> 00:06:14.866
create in the test tube would now be present in the chromosomes of the living cell.

00:06:14.867 --> 00:06:20.366
So that was the intent and we...that was what we wanted to do right away,

00:06:20.367 --> 00:06:25.266
and also we actually even wanted to do it in mice.

00:06:25.267 --> 00:06:27.899
Unfortunately, it took about ten years to develop this.

00:06:27.900 --> 00:06:32.032
So we knew what we wanted to do, we simply didn't know how to get there.

00:06:32.033 --> 00:06:38.566
And in retrospect, what we're taking advantage of is a machine

00:06:38.567 --> 00:06:46.332
that normally repairs DNA. For example, sunlight or oxygen radicals

00:06:46.333 --> 00:06:50.699
that are produced by mitochondria or whatever are destroying the DNA

00:06:50.700 --> 00:06:53.899
and they make, for example, a double strand break.

00:06:53.900 --> 00:06:57.866
So what the cell first does is just jam the DNA back together again

00:06:57.867 --> 00:06:59.932
so that we're not losing thousands of genes

00:06:59.933 --> 00:07:05.066
that are distal, for example, to the centromere which is required to segregate those genes.

00:07:05.067 --> 00:07:10.732
However, at the junction where those two pieces of DNA were stuck back together again,

00:07:10.733 --> 00:07:15.699
a gene is destroyed.  However, fortunately we have two copies of this gene,

00:07:15.700 --> 00:07:21.166
one from your mother and one from your father, if say your mother's copy was destroyed

00:07:21.167 --> 00:07:26.032
then it can use the information from your father's copy

00:07:26.033 --> 00:07:29.532
to correct that and that's by homologous recombination.

00:07:29.533 --> 00:07:33.466
So that's the machinery we're taking advantage of and it's simply

00:07:33.467 --> 00:07:36.232
present in every cell of the body.

00:07:36.233 --> 00:07:41.432
So what we had to do is figure out how this machinery worked

00:07:41.433 --> 00:07:46.932
and then present our DNA to the cell in such a way that it would think it's the right copy

00:07:46.933 --> 00:07:50.266
and thereby convert, essentially the copy that's in the genome

00:07:50.267 --> 00:07:55.132
with the exogenous copy that we're adding from the outside that we've modified.

00:07:55.133 --> 00:07:58.499
And that took about ten years to figure out how to do it.

00:07:58.500 --> 00:08:03.766
Now the other thing that was not apparent right away

00:08:03.767 --> 00:08:07.932
was how to then go from cells to mice.

00:08:07.933 --> 00:08:12.399
And we knew, roughly, how we wanted to do it

00:08:12.400 --> 00:08:16.499
but unfortunately the cells that we required were

00:08:16.500 --> 00:08:20.466
embryonic stem cells from the mouse and they didn't exist at that time.

00:08:20.467 --> 00:08:27.866
And then this is... so now we're roughly in the 1980's

00:08:27.867 --> 00:08:36.399
In 1984, we already presented data to say, "Well, now we want to do gene targeting...

00:08:36.400 --> 00:08:40.166
we do gene targeting in cells." We submitted a grant to the NIH.

00:08:40.167 --> 00:08:45.766
The NIH found that project not possible.

00:08:45.767 --> 00:08:50.932
They said, "The probability, essentially, of your piece of DNA ever being able to find

00:08:50.933 --> 00:08:56.099
that same sequence in 3 billion base pairs is impossible.

00:08:56.100 --> 00:09:00.199
I mean, the frequency would be much to low and therefore it'd never function."

00:09:00.200 --> 00:09:07.599
And we realized that the frequency was going to be low and so what we were thinking about

00:09:07.600 --> 00:09:12.566
is simply developing as a part of a selection. An example would be

00:09:12.567 --> 00:09:19.332
we have a defective gene copy already in the genome, and we'll add a copy of that same

00:09:19.333 --> 00:09:24.066
gene with a different defect. Either one by itself would not be functional

00:09:24.067 --> 00:09:28.032
but together, by homologous recombination, they could recombine in such a way

00:09:28.033 --> 00:09:30.266
that now they would give you a functional copy

00:09:30.267 --> 00:09:34.466
because there are different mutations on those separate genes.

00:09:34.467 --> 00:09:39.966
And so that allows...and if that gene is required for the cell to survive, then you have a very

00:09:39.967 --> 00:09:45.199
strong selection that may work...be able to pick up events, one in a million or so.

00:09:45.200 --> 00:09:48.899
And so that's the way we were approaching it.

00:09:48.900 --> 00:09:54.232
But still, they were skeptical, they gave us money actually for other projects

00:09:54.233 --> 00:10:00.866
and what we did was to utilize that money to continue our effort in gene targeting.

00:10:00.867 --> 00:10:04.766
And fortunately, four years later, we had information that

00:10:04.767 --> 00:10:10.232
it actually was working. We sent the grant back to the same granting agency

00:10:10.233 --> 00:10:14.866
and they sent back a pink slip that said we're glad you didn't follow our advice.

00:10:14.867 --> 00:10:22.366
So, that gives you an idea that, you know, if you have confidence in a particular idea

00:10:22.367 --> 00:10:26.999
go for it and see whether you can come through. It's also risky in the sense that

00:10:27.000 --> 00:10:32.999
if four years later, we hadn't had any results we would have been in deep trouble.

00:10:33.000 --> 00:10:38.366
and unable to obtain other grants simply because we had utilized

00:10:38.367 --> 00:10:40.932
those funds for something that didn't work.

00:10:40.933 --> 00:10:46.932
Fortunately, four years later, we were successful and the project continued.

00:10:46.933 --> 00:10:52.732
The other aspect is, you know, how do you go from cell culture to making mice?

00:10:52.733 --> 00:11:00.666
And for this, at the time, the most attractive cells were called EC cells,

00:11:00.667 --> 00:11:07.432
embryonal carcinoma cells. And those are...it's a tumor essentially, that's made up of many multiple

00:11:07.433 --> 00:11:12.732
cell types and but, within them are stem cells, stem like cells

00:11:12.733 --> 00:11:16.566
in the sense that they could contribute to the formation of multiple different tissues.

00:11:16.567 --> 00:11:23.066
And so I was going from meeting to meeting looking at how the progress was being made

00:11:23.067 --> 00:11:27.899
with EC cells and it was sort of disappointing in a sense that it was working

00:11:27.900 --> 00:11:32.466
to contribute to tissues of the body, what we call somatic cells,

00:11:32.467 --> 00:11:37.632
but it wasn't contributing to the germ line. And for us we wanted to go into the germ line,

00:11:37.633 --> 00:11:40.366
because then, if we ever made a modification,

00:11:40.367 --> 00:11:43.966
we could then generate as many mice as we want

00:11:43.967 --> 00:11:46.432
with that modification simply by breeding.

00:11:46.433 --> 00:11:53.499
But those cells didn't exist.  And then, fortunately, at around 1980...late 1984

00:11:53.500 --> 00:11:59.399
I heard rumors that Martin Evans in Cambridge, England had actually started developing

00:11:59.400 --> 00:12:04.266
cells that may work. At that time he called them EK cells

00:12:04.267 --> 00:12:10.832
and those cells, what he did is to isolate, rather than isolating these cells from a tumor

00:12:10.833 --> 00:12:14.332
he isolated very similar cells from an embryo.

00:12:14.333 --> 00:12:20.166
And simply used EC cells as the driving force to say, "What kind of cells I want."

00:12:20.167 --> 00:12:26.266
but now instead of deriving it from a tumor he was isolating them from the embryo themselves

00:12:26.267 --> 00:12:31.766
and those cells looked like they may be capable of contributing to the germ line

00:12:31.767 --> 00:12:36.099
and therefore would be a suitable substrate for us to do gene targeting with.

00:12:36.100 --> 00:12:43.032
So I called up Martin Evans, this was Christmas of, now, 1985 and

00:12:43.033 --> 00:12:47.166
asked him if I could go to his lab to learn how to work with these cells, and he was

00:12:47.167 --> 00:12:51.466
very generous and allowed us to go.  My wife and I went there,

00:12:51.467 --> 00:12:55.132
spent several weeks learning how to work with these cells,

00:12:55.133 --> 00:13:01.099
how to use those cells, then actually how to make embryos in a sense of introducing them

00:13:01.100 --> 00:13:04.532
to what we call a blastocyst, a pre-implantation embryo

00:13:04.533 --> 00:13:11.632
and then these EK cells are now called ES cells, would then contribute to the formation

00:13:11.633 --> 00:13:16.732
of the embryo proper, once we implant it into the mouse

00:13:16.733 --> 00:13:20.499
but fortunately now these cells were contributing to the germ line.

00:13:20.500 --> 00:13:23.166
So they were perfect for what we wanted to do

00:13:23.167 --> 00:13:26.132
in terms of modifying mice.

00:13:26.133 --> 00:13:30.299
So that essentially gave you the background for us to be able to then, not only

00:13:30.300 --> 00:13:35.899
go from directing DNA at a particular target in cell culture

00:13:35.900 --> 01:00:00.000
but now extending it to formation of mice with specific mutations.

